$\S$8}}~Links; {\inweblinktoanchor{para2_md_9}{
$\S$9}}~Code extracts; {\inweblinktoanchor{para2_md_10}{
$\S$10}}~Images and gadgets; {\inweblinktoanchor{para2_md_11}{
$\S$11}}~Carousels; {\inweblinktoanchor{para2_md_12}{
$\S$12}}~A gratuitously long table; {\inweblinktoanchor{para2_md_13}{
$\S$13}}~Index}\par\medskip\hrule\bigskip
\followsheadingtrue
\weavesections{1}{Commentary}{Chapter 2: Commentary -- Markdown Demonstration\quad$\S$1}{\S}{2/md}%
\inwebanchor{para2_md_1}\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
The content of this section is again meaningless. It exists only to test
the renderers.

\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
Here is some \emph{emphasis} and \textbf{strong emphasis} and \strikethrough{text struck through}.

\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
Here is some |literal backticked text|.

\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
Some index entries appear here, invisibly.


\weavesections{2}{Character set}{Chapter 2: Commentary -- Markdown Demonstration\quad$\S$2}{\S}{2/md}%
\inwebanchor{para2_md_2}\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
These are potentially tricky for TeX: $\{$Knuth$\}$. A\textbackslash{}B. A\_B. A\#B. \$40. A\|B. 30\%. 2\char`^56.

\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
And in backticked literal text: |{Knuth}. A\B. A_B. A#B. $40. A|{\tt\|}|B. 30%. 2^26.|

\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
These are potentially tricky for HTML: {\tt<}, \&, {\tt>}.

\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
And in backticked literal text: |<, &, >|.

\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
``Double quoted.'' `Single quoted.'

\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
In TeX variants, it's `reasonable' to hope for ``smart quotes'' to be imposed, but
more questionable whether phrases like église château mañana will be accented.


\weavesections{3}{ISO Latin-1}{Chapter 2: Commentary -- Markdown Demonstration\quad$\S$3}{\S}{2/md}%
\inwebanchor{para2_md_3}\iffollowsheading\par\noindent\fi\followsheadingfalse
Some formats can take most of Unicode, but others are more limited, and plain TeX
is stuck with a small selection of ASCII, leaving the following ragged. It's in
both tabular and untabular form because of issues with TeX rendering of table cells,
which mean that implementation of magic characters works differently in those cases.


